# Broad reference is the grammar term for a `this` or `which` that
# points at a whole earlier statement instead of at one noun (`This
# means the cache stays warm`, `, which means the pool is empty`). The
# reader has to pick which part of the last sentence the pronoun names.
# The fix names the noun (`This retry means`) or joins the two claims.
#
# `this` counts only before a verb that states a consequence. `This is`
# alone also names a field or a section (`this is set to True`), so it
# counts only before `because`, `why`, or `how`. The allows rule
# reports `allows you to`, `lets you`, `gives you`, and `makes it
# possible`, so a guard glued to each verb skips those. `helps` is left
# out, because the chatbot rule reports `hope this helps` and the
# allows rule reports `helps to` and `helps you`.
#
# It hit 203 lines in the kubernetes and nix.dev docs, and 10 of 10
# sampled hits pointed at a whole clause.
expr verb [means, meant, ensures, happens, prevents, causes, keeps, works, matters, (makes!(( )it)), (allows!(( )[you, users])), (lets!(( )[you, users])), (gives!(( )you))]
expr main [(this @verb), (this [is, was] [because, why, how]), ((,) which means)]
let message "Broad reference. Name the noun it points at, or join the two claims."
let description "Flags this and which used as a pronoun for a whole earlier clause."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "This retry means the cache stays warm."
allows "This field holds the name."
allows "This change fixes the race."
allows "This lets you pin the version."
allows "Hope this helps."
allows "For a Pod, this is set to True before the sandbox starts."
